{"answer":"No. The algebraic argument is a valid proof by contradiction, but its claimed conclusion has the direction reversed. Assuming sqrt(2)=a/b in lowest terms gives a^2=2b^2. Since an even square has an even base, a is even; writing a=2k then gives b^2=2k^2, so b is even as well. Thus 2 divides both a and b, contradicting lowest terms. Consequently no lowest-terms integer quotient can equal sqrt(2), and sqrt(2) is irrational (using the standard fact that a rational number has such a representation).","approaches":[{"description":"Assume a lowest-terms representation and derive that both numerator and denominator are divisible by 2.","id":"contradiction-route","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It establishes a contradiction with the assumption, hence refutes rather than proves the assumed rationality."},{"description":"Read the displayed equations as direct evidence that sqrt(2) is rational.","id":"affirm-rationality","outcome":"rejected","reason":"The equations arise only under the rationality assumption, and their consequence contradicts the lowest-terms condition."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question states: \"write sqrt(2)=a/b in lowest terms, square to get a^2=2b^2\".","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"assumed-representation","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["derive-even-numerator","derive-even-denominator","lowest-terms-contradiction"],"statement":"For contradiction, assume sqrt(2)=a/b for integers a,b with b nonzero and with a/b in lowest terms; then a^2=2b^2.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the parity argument in derive-even-numerator.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"numerator-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the assumed representation, a is even.","step_ids":["derive-even-numerator"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived after substituting a=2k in derive-even-denominator.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["numerator-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"denominator-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the assumed representation, b is even.","step_ids":["derive-even-denominator"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in lowest-terms-contradiction.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["numerator-even","denominator-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":["lowest-terms-contradiction"],"id":"representation-contradiction","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The assumed lowest-terms representation is impossible, because 2 divides both a and b.","step_ids":["lowest-terms-contradiction"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"standard_result","basis_reference":"Lowest-terms representation theorem for rational numbers: every rational number can be expressed as a/b with integers a,b, b nonzero, and gcd(a,b)=1; applied in infer-irrationality.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["representation-contradiction"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"sqrt-two-irrational","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"sqrt(2) is irrational.","step_ids":["infer-irrationality"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The contradiction established under the rationality assumption yields its negation, not the assumption itself.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["representation-contradiction","sqrt-two-irrational"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claimed-conclusion-false","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The proof's claimed conclusion that sqrt(2) is rational does not follow; the argument instead supports irrationality.","step_ids":["assess-logical-direction"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"derive-even-numerator","justification":"From a^2=2b^2, a^2 is even. If a were odd, a=2r+1 for some integer r, so a^2=4r(r+1)+1 would be odd. Hence a is even.","statement":"Establish that a is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["derive-even-numerator"],"id":"derive-even-denominator","justification":"Write a=2k. Substitution into a^2=2b^2 gives 4k^2=2b^2, hence b^2=2k^2. The same odd-square argument used in derive-even-numerator shows that b is even.","statement":"Establish that b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["derive-even-numerator","derive-even-denominator"],"id":"lowest-terms-contradiction","justification":"The preceding steps give 2|a and 2|b. Thus a and b have the common divisor 2, contrary to the scoped lowest-terms assumption.","statement":"Derive the contradiction with lowest terms."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["lowest-terms-contradiction"],"id":"infer-irrationality","justification":"Apply the lowest-terms representation theorem: if sqrt(2) were rational, it would admit the representation assumed in assumed-representation. But lowest-terms-contradiction rules out every such representation.","statement":"Conclude that sqrt(2) is irrational."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["lowest-terms-contradiction","infer-irrationality"],"id":"assess-logical-direction","justification":"A proof by contradiction assumes a proposition and derives an impossibility; it establishes the proposition's negation. Here the assumed proposition is rationality, so the argument cannot establish rationality.","statement":"Assess the direction of the stated proof."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
